  "detail": "Thank you: token handling, confinement of repo.git (not writable by either harness's session) and the lease all held up in review. Two publication gaps remain, both reproduced against a local remote. (a) Only `parentSha..sha` is scanned, but pushing `sha` also publishes every ancestor the remote doesn't have. A private string in the parent's commit message reached the remote with `expectedRemoteSha: null`. (b) The branch name is published without any check. A push to `limitless/<private string>` succeeded with that string on the list and `discreet: true`. The coordinator's delivery (src/pipeline/engine.ts around line 2270) checks both; the worker must do the same, reusing the same functions rather than new ones. Required: (1) Add `baseSha` (the run's base commit) to `AgentJobRequest`, the lease projection and the worker schema, additively like the other publication fields. (2) Before the push, check the branch name with `checkPublicationText(branch, \"Branch name\", entries)`. (3) Compute the range with `publicationRange(cwd, baseSha, sha, expectedRemoteSha)` from src/git/repos.ts, which the coordinator uses, and run `checkPrivateRange`, the registered-credential check and (in discreet mode) `checkDiscreetCommits` on that range instead of `parentSha..sha`. If `expectedRemoteSha` is not present in the job's repository, refuse (the coordinator must send it in the bundle; that is S5's job). (4) In discreet mode, refuse an unpublished factory-marked branch name (starts with `limitless/` or contains the run id, with `expectedRemoteSha` null), the same rule as `deliveryPublicationRange` (src/git/repos.ts around line 989). Move that rule into one small shared helper that both `deliveryPublicationRange` and the worker call, so the two can't drift; don't change the coordinator's behaviour otherwise. Tests: a private string in an ancestor's message, in an ancestor's diff, and in the branch name each refuse with no push; a factory-marked unpublished branch in discreet mode refuses; a branch whose remote head is an ancestor scans only from that head."
